`20. Abandonment and items left behind`,`Landlord treats the Home as abandoned only when Landlord reasonably believes Tenant has left: for example, keys returned, belongings removed, or nobody living there, together with unpaid rent. Before taking possession without a court order, Landlord posts a written notice on the door (and sends one to Tenant's last known address) as Louisiana law requires. In a parish under a federal disaster declaration, Tenant's absence during the first 30 days after the declaration is never treated as abandonment. Items left after Tenant moves out or is lawfully evicted may be removed, stored, and disposed of after any notice the law requires. Reasonable costs may be deducted from the deposit.`),o(`21. Notices and electronic signatures`,`- Everyday matters (repair requests, entry notices, reminders) may be sent by text or email: Landlord ${r(`landlord_contact`)}; Tenant ${r(`tenant_contact`)}.`,`- Formal notices (ending the lease, notice to vacate, default, acceleration, right to cure, eviction, foreclosure) are given on paper: hand-delivered, mailed, or attached to the door when the law allows. Landlord's address: ${r(`landlord_address`)}. Tenant's address: the Home (or the forwarding address after move-out). A copy may also be sent electronically.`,`- Notice to or from any one Tenant counts for all Tenants.`,`- The parties agree to sign this lease and its addenda electronically. Each party may still ask for paper for any later document.`),o(`22. Foreclosure disclosure`,`Tenant has the right to receive written notice of any foreclosure action against the Home.`,t.foreclosure===`pending`?`The Home is subject to a pending foreclosure action: ${r(`foreclosure_details`)}.`:`The Home is not currently subject to any pending foreclosure action.`,`If Landlord is ever served with a notice of seizure in a foreclosure of the Home, Landlord will, within 7 calendar days, give every Tenant a signed written notice naming the court, case name and docket 